{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"linty-sunshiny-gull","creation_channel":"python","description":"## The problem\n\nTicket 330c is understood to normalize AC CHECKBOX STATE out of the plan-review material\nfingerprint, so ticking `- [ ]` to `- [x]` at close time should be attestation-SAFE. Observed\nbehavior on 2026-08-05 does not match that consistently, and the close workflow depends on it:\nthe close gate REQUIRES every AC ticked, while a stale attestation BLOCKS the close. If ticking\ncan invalidate, the two requirements are in direct tension and the author is stuck.\n\n## Three observations, same day, same repo\n\n1. **Ticking was SAFE.** The agent on bug 846b-1bb5-4cd3-4249 verified explicitly rather than\n   assuming: after ticking, `review-plan --status` still read `certified` with an UNCHANGED\n   `signed_at`. It reported this as a correction to contrary guidance.\n\n2. **Ticking WITH added evidence prose INVALIDATED.** The agent on task c6c9-cba0-1aa4-47eb\n   reported `stale-material` on close after checking boxes with evidence text, and resolved it\n   by re-running `review-plan`.\n\n3. **Plain ticking INVALIDATED.** The agent on bug 1909-c1a7-9f20-440f reported\n   `stale-material` with reason \"description/AC/file_impact/children changed\" after ticking,\n   explicitly noting this contradicted the 330c guidance it had been given. It remediated with\n   a fresh `review-plan` (PASS, re-certified, 0 blocking). No `--force`.\n\nObservations 1 and 3 appear to conflict directly. Candidate explanations, none verified:\n- the normalization covers only some edit shapes (e.g. state flip but not surrounding\n  whitespace/reflow), and one agent's edit incidentally changed more than the marker;\n- the fingerprint differs by ticket TYPE or by which profile (claim vs close) is evaluated;\n- `file_impact` or `children` changed concurrently and the reason string names all four\n  material inputs without distinguishing which actually differed.\n\nThat last one is worth checking first: the reported reason is a fixed list —\n\"description/AC/file_impact/children changed\" — so it may not indicate the AC edit at all.\nIf so, the defect is DIAGNOSTIC rather than behavioral, and the fix is to name the input that\nactually differed.\n\n## Why it matters beyond confusion\n\nThis guidance was actively propagated to four independent agents in one session, on the basis\nof observation 1. Agents that trust it and are then blocked will suspect a gate malfunction; the\ntempting escape is `--force-close`, which is precisely the wrong lesson. Getting a definitive\nanswer is cheap; leaving it ambiguous quietly erodes the close gate's authority.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The actual behavior is determined empirically: does flipping ONLY a checkbox marker,\n      with no other byte changed, alter the material fingerprint? Answer yes or no with a\n      reproduction.\n- [ ] If it does NOT, the three observations are reconciled — identify what else changed in\n      cases 2 and 3 (most likely candidate: the reason string names all material inputs\n      without distinguishing which differed).\n- [ ] If the diagnostic is the problem, the staleness reason NAMES THE INPUT THAT ACTUALLY\n      CHANGED rather than listing every possibility. A reader must not have to guess.\n- [ ] The behavior is documented where AC authors and closing agents will read it, stating\n      plainly whether ticking is safe.\n- [ ] If ticking genuinely does invalidate, the tension with the close gate's require-all-ticked\n      rule is resolved rather than left for each author to rediscover.\n\n## Provenance\n\nThree independent agent reports on 2026-08-05, cited above. Related: bug 858c-786a-13be-4332\ncovers a different defect in the same area — the close gate cannot express an N/A criterion,\nforcing a tick that falsely asserts completion.\n","id":"94a3-1323-3497-4f59","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Whether ticking an AC checkbox invalidates a plan-review attestation is inconsistent across tickets"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785917731798600001,"uuid":"34e8aac7-fb8e-4c62-9921-58fdb28e1183"}